Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.3. Superior Proposal Considerations. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, the Company Board may determine an Acquisition Proposal to be a Superior Proposal if, and only if, (i) the Company Board concludes in good faith, after consultation with its financial advisor and outside legal counsel, that the failure to consider the Acquisition Proposal would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable Law, (ii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board’s intention to consider the Acquisition Proposal at least five Business Days prior to making any determination, and (iii) the Company Board has taken into account the financial terms of the proposal, the strategic benefits, the potential impact on employees, customers, and other stakeholders, and any other relevant factors deemed appropriate by the Company Board. Additionally, the Company Board shall engage in good faith negotiations with Parent during the notice period to allow Parent to propose amendments to the current Agreement to match or exceed the terms of the Acquisition Proposal. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
Choices:

